<http://data.silknow.org/object/043c10e2-990c-301d-9b1c-32fda5c21937>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "The double-headed eagle became the primary symbol of the state during the late Byzantine centuries and was also adopted for liturgical use. This huge eagle was probably used as an altar cloth or as a podea, a skirt hung beneath an icon. The inscription, which connects the owner with distinguished imperial dynasties, exaggerated the claims of a pretender to the patriarchal throne."@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/b6753548-320a-34b8-ac13-3d8b7b496384> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/4f05f67b-6db8-30e2-b0a8-39c584208b6d> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/f7ee99c5-af54-3616-a1b6-30ee00c4c01d> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/2bab192e-468c-3d1d-bab9-1784b02f7928> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/6385ea0d-f125-3074-bd38-814a89615b4f> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "The double-headed eagle became the primary symbol of the state during the late Byzantine centuries and was also adopted for liturgical use. This huge eagle was probably used as an altar cloth or as a podea, a skirt hung beneath an icon. The inscription, which connects the owner with distinguished imperial dynasties, exaggerated the claims of a pretender to the patriarchal throne."@en ;
